professor adam finn from the university  of bristol sits on the jcbi that's the  government's advisory group on vaccines  who joins us now  um it feels as if the vaccine is in a  race  against the new variant of the virus  who's winning the race right now  uh well i think that's a good analogy  we're in a race against time  and that explains uh the way the  vaccine's being deployed  it's really too soon for the vaccine to  have started having a measurable  impact but we can predict that already  it's present  it's preventing cases just simply from  the numbers  of people who've received the vaccine  which is now approaching one and a half  million people  and the rate of infection that's  occurring which is really very high now  so there are certainly thousands of  people already who have not been  admitted to hospital and who will not be  dying of this infection as a consequence  of the program that's begun in december  and matt hancock was saying that the  delays if you like to getting the  vaccine rolled out even more quickly is  about  supply i just wonder if that's your  understanding why can't we get out more  quickly is it supply  is it bureaucracy is it the actual roll  out with gps what is it  well i have to confess i'm not very  close to the organization of the  operation  so i i know about this as much as you in  terms of what's reported in the media  in terms of what's going on locally in  bristol we're certainly now receiving  supplies of vaccine  and seeing increasing numbers of people  receiving those  doses and i i understand that  getting an operation on this scale going  does take a certain amount of time but  it's  it seems to be ramping up very quickly  as far as i can  establish uh yes does certainly start to  seem to be starting to increase and the  house secretary also said that they hope  to start to be able to lift  restrictions by spring do you think  that's realistic  well i think the the strategy that we've  advised the government to take which is  to  immunise people who are at the highest  risk of becoming sick  with the infection as well as frontline  healthcare workers who are responsible  for caring for them to increase  resilience of the nhs should  result in less pressure if you like  to shut things down simply because the  primary driver of the shutdown at the  moment is the pressure on the nhs  and the concerns that we could be  confronting a situation where  sick people who can be effectively  treated and not  able to be treated for lack of people  and resources to do that  so i do think that we will see an impact  on  those pressures but i think he was  correct  in pointing out that immunizing these  people will not  stop the circulation of the virus which  is going on throughout  the population so there will continue to  be concerns about that beyond this first  phase  um you're saying about the lift that you  do think there is going to be an impact  on reducing the pressure on the nhs  and so on and but when do you think this  is going to  start to happen i mean how many people  will we have to vaccinate for that to  happen and when do you think we might  hit that number  well it'll be a progressive thing as i  said at the beginning  i think it's already happening to an  extent but of course it's happening in  the context of an epidemic that's going  up  so we might be seeing a slight reduction  in the number of cases but they're still  going up  what we should see if all goes to plan  is that that will  as well it'll play a part in reducing  that rate of rise and eventually  help bring it down but it is quite  difficult to predict exactly how soon  that will happen  because it's not just the vaccine that's  influencing the number of cases  it's also people's behavior their number  of contacts and the infectiousness of  this  new variant so very tricky to predict  that with very uh  with very much certainty at this point  but as each week goes by  we'll be able to predict it with  increasing confidence  so i suspect if you brought me on this  program in a month i could answer that  question more accurately than i can now  okay we'll we'll hold you to that we'll  we'll bring you on in a month and we'll  see  how we're getting on at that stage  definitely and you talked about  compliance  how worried are you that people are not  following the rules and what kind of  thing do you think they might be doing  to break them  uh well again this is not something that  i'm that close to  i mean i can observe what's going on  around me here in bristol  and certainly there are a lot of people  out and about walking and  you can see behind me i'm on the harbour  side here it's a very popular place for  people to come out  and spend time so my personal impression  is that  people are perhaps not being so careful  as they might be to keep well apart at  the moment which is  a very imperative need but  on the other hand this latest lockdown  has only just  uh started so the impact of the lockdown  would be expected to be seen in another  week's time  okay um now there's lots of questions  about  uh the recommendations jcbi has given  not least on the spacing of the doses  because you're saying  that the second dose can be delayed for  up to 12 weeks now pfizer  of course the makers of one of the  vaccines has said there are no data to  demonstrate that protection after the  first dose  is sustained after 21 days  i mean do you not think you need a bit  more evidence than just thinking well  the other vaccines seem to work okay  after uh  12 weeks so let's take the fisa one does  too  uh yeah i think in normal times you  would absolutely  wait for more evidence and we're busy  collecting that evidence  already as you can imagine because there  are plenty of people  now who've had one dose and we can  observe their immune responses  and the effectiveness over the coming  couple of months  but this is not normal times and  we what we have to do is extrapolate  from the data we've got  based on the knowledge that we have of  many other vaccines and the human immune  response  and make a strategic judgment because  the truth is that if you  take a vaccine dose that you're  otherwise saving to give to somebody as  a second booster dose  and put it into the arm of someone who's  not immunized at all  you will start to induce protection in  that second person  and with this rapidly rising epidemic at  the moment that is the imperative need  uh essentially if your house is on fire  you've got two fire extinguishers you  use them both  uh and you rely on there being another  fire extinguisher to obtain ready for  the next fire  you don't wait and plan in the way that  you would  uh when you're not in the middle of a  crisis  is that i completely understand the  balance of risk here  and the need to get things out quickly  particularly in the face of this new  variant  but is there a risk though that the  fires of vaccine could be  less effective if spaced out to this  amount  well we know that the uh the  effectiveness between the first and the  second dose is actually extraordinarily  high  it's one of the few bits of good luck  we've had in  recent times that these early vaccines  are  really very very effective and therefore  it's very implausible that that just  that protection would would  kind of go off a cliff would suddenly  disappear over the subsequent few weeks  we simply don't see that in other  vaccines and other diseases  and although the two different vaccines  have a different means  of of working they're basically both  resulting in expression of the spike  protein  and inducing the same immune response so  there is  actually plenty of logic to  extrapolating from one to the other  particularly  weighing up as you you clearly  understand that the risks  against the benefits which are  absolutely concrete we  we know that by giving this vaccine to  people rather than keeping it in the  freezer  it's inducing a benefit straight away  there's also a lot of concern um about  some of the new variants  and the impact this could have on the  vaccine particularly the  variant we're calling the south africa  variant and  what does the evidence suggest we know  of course that fires have said that they  believe that their vaccine will be  uh effective against the south african  africa variant  do we know anything about the oxford  astrozenica vaccine as well  well this evidence is just starting to  arrive and there's there's  one particular mutation in both variants  which is in the part of the spike  protein which binds  to human cells which was a particular  concern  and we received the evidence yesterday  from  pfizer that a virus with that particular  mutation on its own  does not reduce it does not escape if  you like from the immunity induced by  their vaccine  so as far as it goes that's a very  encouraging first step  and i think we will see over the coming  week or  the next few days more evidence on these  two variants  hopefully confirming that so so far so  good  uh i think we all think that the  vaccines will continue to work  and we all they were shown in the trials  uh but  uh we we're not quite 100 certain of  that yet  fingers crossed um talking about the  variants of course the uk scientists  picked up  the variant in kent we've got excellent  sequencing  in this country south africa picked up  on the variant emerging in their country  and we know  of course that they've got a very good  modelling program there too because of  the history  with hiv are you worried that there  could be  other serious variants in other  countries that we just simply don't know  about yet  well i think we know that this virus  varies and it was already varying  before these two particular variants  were described  and that's going to continue to happen i  think it would be better if other  countries had more  resources to do the genotyping and it  may well be that  these variants are circulating more  widely than we know about simply because  people have not been able to look but  looking forwards i think we have to  anticipate the likelihood actually that  there will be further variation  and that in due course we'll have to  modify the vaccines to sort of keep up  with the virus  that that's pretty much an expectation  but it's something that we can do  and the more people are able to monitor  the variations going on around the world  the more quickly we'll be able to do  that yeah it's quite interesting to hear  matt hancock say earlier in the program  that people could expect to get the flu  vaccine and the covert vaccine  you need for the foreseeable future to  protect them as things uh  mutate um just finally the jcvi has  recommended that  the most vulnerable uh get priority to  the vaccine first which of course  everyone can understand that's the most  likely to bring down  the death rate as quickly as possible is  there an argument though  that some key workers should be  prioritized i'm thinking particularly of  teachers to get schools reopened yeah  yeah as a pediatrician i'm  extremely keen that we can try and  reopen schools and then keep them open  um and absolutely that's the next phase  of  the program and jcvi will be discussing  over the coming months  uh phase two if you like as to who  should be prioritized  now these considerations start to be  uh social values in a way more than the  criteria we normally use which is  pressure on the health service  there are broader considerations when it  comes to people with different  occupations and the relative importance  of them in society but  i would certainly be a strong advocate  for schools and teachers  so you think that jc jcvi could  recommend that teachers get moved up the  queue then  quite soon well i i i can't predict  exactly what will be prioritized but i  can say that we will be  discussing this and coming up with a  plan and i can also say that  when it comes to teachers i think we all  appreciate the critical role that they  all play  and so that really will figure in the  discussions and when one of those  discussions happening  uh well we're meeting next thursday  we're meeting every week at the moment  and uh our chair has uh instructed us  that we  we must come up with a a phase two plan  by the middle of february  uh really interesting to talk thank you  very much for letting us know that'd be  welcome news to  some teachers i can imagine as well  thank you for being on  pleasure nice to talk

